        "text": "{\"verdict\":\"fail\",\"reasons\":[\"Scope mismatch: § 11.3 is an acceptable-use/content-warranty enforcement right (IBM may investigate suspected unlawful, infringing or abusive Content and then remove/suspend), not a usage or fee-compliance audit. The AUDIT rule is framed around verification of our usage with an underpayment true-up ('independent auditor', 'underpayment exceeds 5%/10%'), so importing it wholesale here misapplies the rule; the absence of any true usage/licence audit right is favourable to us and should not be raised as a deviation.\",\"The imported cost mechanic is incoherent as drafted: 'at IBM's cost unless the investigation reveals an underpayment by Customer exceeding ten percent (10%) of amounts due' has no referent in a § 11.2(b) content-warranty investigation, which cannot produce an underpayment finding. The insertion adds confusing, inoperative language rather than the smallest effective edit.\",\"The redline imposes an absolute 15-day prior written notice condition on any investigation without preserving the existing emergency / 'as required by law' carve-out reflected in § 11.3's notice provision (p0137). As written it would bar IBM from investigating suspected illegal content promptly or where law prohibits tipping off the Customer — an internally inconsistent and non-negotiable position that also invites counterparty rejection of the whole guardrail package.\",\"The once-per-twelve-month frequency cap is unworkable for abuse investigations (it would prevent IBM investigating a second suspected incident in the same year) and is not what the playbook's 'rare, notified, off-site' audit position is designed to address; the confidentiality/independent-investigator and no-system-access asks are the only parts that map sensibly to this clause.\",\"If a comment is warranted at all, it should be a narrower ask (advance notice where practicable, confidentiality obligations on investigators, no access to Customer systems beyond what is necessary), not a full audit-clause template rewrite of an AUP enforcement right.\"],\"severityAdjustment\":\"low\"}"
      }
